        "2": {
            "name": "text_block",
            "id": 311,
            "position_x": 862,
            "position_y": 45,
            "width": 768,
            "height": 1016,
            "visibility": "show_all",
            "draggable": true,
            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Finds the separate regions - blobs - in a grid: groups of neighbouring cells above a threshold, each group cut off from the others. It numbers them 1, 2, 3 ..., and gives how many there are, the size of each and the centre of each.\n\nThe centres make it a simple tracker: the blobs in a depth or occupancy grid are the people in a room; in a thresholded camera image, the bright objects; in a heat map of activity, the hot spots. The sizes let you ignore specks.\n\nSYNTAX:\nscipy.label <threshold: float>\nDefault: threshold 0.5.\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nThe grid (usually 2-D; any number of axes works). Every input is searched and sent. A true/false mask is used as it is; numbers count where they are above the threshold.\n\nthreshold (float box, also an inlet):\nThe level a cell must be above to count as part of a blob.\n\ndiagonals connect:\nAn option, under the node. Off (the default), cells join a blob only side by side; on, corner to corner too - two blobs touching only at a corner become one.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nlabels:\nThe grid with each cell numbered by its blob: 0 for background, 1 to count for the blobs.\n\ncount:\nHow many blobs.\n\nsizes:\nHow many cells in each blob, blob 1 first.\n\ncentres:\nThe centre of each blob, one row each: row then column, in cells. Sent first, then sizes, count and labels.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nRandom values smoothed with scipy.gaussian_filter make soft hills (left). Cut at 0.55, the hilltops become separate blobs, each a colour (right); the count and the centres change with each new picture, every two seconds.\n\nRELATED:\nscipy.gaussian_filter smooths a grid first, so its blobs are shapes and not specks. np.histogram2d makes an occupancy grid from points. np.argwhere lists every cell above a threshold.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 1000,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Finds the separate regions - blobs - in a grid: groups of neighbouring cells above a threshold, each group cut off from the others. It numbers them 1, 2, 3 ..., and gives how many there are, the size of each and the centre of each.\n\nThe centres make it a simple tracker: the blobs in a depth or occupancy grid are the people in a room; in a thresholded camera image, the bright objects; in a heat map of activity, the hot spots. The sizes let you ignore specks.\n\nSYNTAX:\nscipy.label <threshold: float>\nDefault: threshold 0.5.\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nThe grid (usually 2-D; any number of axes works). Every input is searched and sent. A true/false mask is used as it is; numbers count where they are above the threshold.\n\nthreshold (float box, also an inlet):\nThe level a cell must be above to count as part of a blob.\n\ndiagonals connect:\nAn option, under the node. Off (the default), cells join a blob only side by side; on, corner to corner too - two blobs touching only at a corner become one.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nlabels:\nThe grid with each cell numbered by its blob: 0 for background, 1 to count for the blobs.\n\ncount:\nHow many blobs.\n\nsizes:\nHow many cells in each blob, blob 1 first.\n\ncentres:\nThe centre of each blob, one row each: row then column, in cells. Sent first, then sizes, count and labels.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nRandom values smoothed with scipy.gaussian_filter make soft hills (left). Cut at 0.55, the hilltops become separate blobs, each a colour (right); the count and the centres change with each new picture, every two seconds.\n\nRELATED:\nscipy.gaussian_filter smooths a grid first, so its blobs are shapes and not specks. np.histogram2d makes an occupancy grid from points. np.argwhere lists every cell above a threshold."
        },
